s geographic position at the eastern slope of the Sierra Nevada mountains and near the Truckee River positioned it as a strategic hub for trade, commerce, and migration, particularly during the silver boom that characterized the late 19th century. The establishment of the Central Pacific Railroad further enhanced Reno's significance as it connected the city to broader markets and facilitated an influx of settlers and entrepreneurs seeking opportunities in the burgeoning Nevada territory.

